window.__finlexDocLoaded(10112,{"id":10112,"articles":[{"id":1000478,"no":"Overview","heading":"Official Overview & Scope (IFRS Foundation)","text":"Investment property is land or a building (including part of a building) or both that is:\n\nheld to earn rentals or for capital appreciation or both;\n\nnot owner-occupied;\n\nnot used in production or supply of goods and services, or for administration; and\n\nnot held for sale in the ordinary course of business.\n\nInvestment property may include investment property that is being redeveloped.\n\nAn investment property is measured initially at cost. The cost of an investment property interest held under a lease is measured in accordance with IAS 17 at the lower of the fair value of the property interest and the present value of the minimum lease payments.\n\nFor subsequent measurement an entity must adopt either the fair value model or the cost model as its accounting policy for all investment properties. All entities must determine fair value for measurement (if the entity uses the fair value model) or disclosure (if it uses the cost model). Fair value reflects market conditions at the end of the reporting period.\n\nUnder the fair value model, investment property is remeasured at the end of each reporting period. Changes in fair value are recognised in profit or loss as they occur. Fair value is the price at which the property could be exchanged between knowledgeable, willing parties in an arm’s length transaction, without deducting transaction costs (see IFRS 13).\n\nUnder the cost model, investment property is measured at cost less accumulated depreciation and any accumulated impairment losses. Fair value is disclosed.\n\nGains and losses on disposal are recognised in profit or loss.","seq":0},{"id":1000479,"no":"IAS 40.5–65 & CAS 3","heading":"Investment Property: Cost vs. Fair Value Model (vs. CAS 3)","text":"• Scope (IAS 40.5–15): land or building held to earn rentals or for capital appreciation (not owner-occupied under IAS 16 or held for sale in ordinary course under IAS 2).\n• Accounting policy choice (IAS 40.30–56): choose EITHER Fair Value Model (fair value changes recognised directly in P&L, NO depreciation) OR Cost Model (depreciated under IAS 16, with fair value still disclosed in notes).\n• Key CAS 3 vs. IAS 40 nuance: China CAS 3 requires Cost Model as default and permits Fair Value Model only when an active local property market provides reliable ongoing fair value evidence, and prohibits switching back from Fair Value to Cost.","seq":1},{"id":1000480,"no":"History 1","heading":"Standard History & Amendments","text":"In April 2001 the International Accounting Standards Board (Board) adopted IAS 40 Investment Property, which had originally been issued by the International Accounting Standards Committee in April 2000. That Standard had replaced some parts of IAS 25 Accounting for Investments, which had been issued in March 1986 and had not already been replaced by IAS 39 Financial Instruments: Recognition and Measurement.\n\nIn December 2003 the Board issued a revised IAS 40 as part of its initial agenda of technical projects.\n\nIn January 2016, IFRS 16 Leases made various amendments to IAS 40, including expanding its scope to include both owned investment property and investment property held by a lessee as a right-of-use asset.","seq":2},{"id":1000481,"no":"History 2","heading":"Standard History & Amendments","text":"In December 2016, the Board issued Transfers of Investment Property (Amendments to IAS 40) which clarifies when there is a transfer to, or from, investment property.\n\nOther Standards have made minor consequential amendments to IAS 40. They include IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement (issued May 2011), Annual Improvements to IFRSs 2011–2013 Cycle (issued December 2013), IFRS 15 Revenue from Contracts with Customers (issued May 2014), Agriculture: Bearer Plants (Amendments to IAS 16 and IAS 41) (issued June 2014), IFRS 17 Insurance Contracts (issued May 2017), Amendments to References to the Conceptual Framework in IFRS Standards (issued March 2018) and IFRS 18 Presentation and Disclosure in Financial Statements (issued April 2024).","seq":3},{"id":1000482,"no":"Support","heading":"Related Projects & Implementation Support","text":"[Related completed projects]\n• Clarifying the Interrelationship of IFRS 3 and IAS 40 when Classifying Property as Investment Property or Owner-occupied Property\n• Transfers of Investment Property (Amendments to IAS 40)\n\n[Related IFRS Standards]\n• IFRS 13 Fair Value Measurement\n\n[Implementation support]\n• IAS 40 supporting material","seq":4},{"id":1000483,"no":"References","heading":"Official Text Link & Public Practical Guidance","text":"Official IFRS Foundation Page (English): https://www.ifrs.org/issued-standards/list-of-standards/ias-40-investment-property/\nAccess Note: Free access to the unaccompanied text of IFRS Accounting Standards on ifrs.org requires a free registered IFRS.org account; Basis for Conclusions and Illustrative Examples require an IFRS Digital Subscription.\nPublic Companion Overview: KPMG Insights into IFRS — An Overview (Sept 2026): https://assets.kpmg.com/content/dam/kpmgsites/xx/pdf/ifrg/2026/isg-2026-insights-an-overview.pdf","seq":5}]});
